"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkelemental_selenium||=[]).push([[4131],{247617(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"locators","permalink":"/tips/tags/locators","allTagsPath":"/tips/tags","count":7,"items":[{"id":"css-vs-xpath/32-css-vs-xpath","title":"CSS vs. Xpath","description":"One of the most heated and subjective conversations in the Selenium community is which locator strategy is better, often circling around two -- XPath and CSS.","permalink":"/tips/32-css-vs-xpath"},{"id":"css-vs-xpath-2/33-css-vs-xpath-2","title":"CSS vs. XPath 2","description":"To get a real taste of what the performance difference is between CSS and XPath, we will need to dig deeper. We\'ll do that by running everything from a local machine while using a performance benchmarking utility.","permalink":"/tips/33-css-vs-xpath-2"},{"id":"css-vs-xpath-3/34-css-vs-xpath-3","title":"CSS vs. XPath 3","description":"In the last tip I provided benchmarks that showed XPath and CSS performance across various browsers. Since then I\'ve received feedback on some things that my test was missing -- most notably more browser coverage and testing against a larger and deeper page.","permalink":"/tips/34-css-vs-xpath-3"},{"id":"highlight-elements/65-highlight-elements","title":"Highlight Elements","description":"It\'s likely that you\'ll run into odd test behavior that makes you question the locators you\'re using in a test. But how do you interrogate your locators to make sure they are doing what you expect?","permalink":"/tips/65-highlight-elements"},{"id":"find-by-role/59-find-by-role","title":"How to Find Elements by Role and Name","description":"IDs and CSS classes change when the page is redesigned, and many widgets don\'t have them. The role and the name of an element, as a screen reader announces them, change less often, and finding elements by them also checks that the page is accessible.","permalink":"/tips/59-find-by-role"},{"id":"shadow-dom/46-shadow-dom","title":"How to Find Elements in the Shadow DOM","description":"Web components keep their elements in a shadow root, apart from the rest of the page, so a normal locator doesn\'t find them. The test has to go through the shadow root, or use a tool whose locators look inside it.","permalink":"/tips/46-shadow-dom"},{"id":"locator-strategy/22-locator-strategy","title":"Locator Strategy","description":"In web design CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is used to apply styles to the markup (HTML) on a page. CSS is able to do this by declaring which bits of the the markup it wants to interact with through the use of selectors.","permalink":"/tips/22-locator-strategy"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);